    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I’ve always had a bit of a fondness for math, mostly from my chemistry classes. It's practical and useful. I’ve never been great at it, but I always enjoy learning more about it. I use it daily in my work as a self-employed designer. It’s more about understanding patterns and solving problems than rote memorization.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Sometimes. Especially when I'm faced with a complex problem I haven’t seen before. It's not crippling, but it can be a little stressful. I usually calm down by taking a break and coming back to it with a fresh mind. I’ve learned to embrace my anxiety as a sign that I’m engaged and thinking deeply about a problem.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used an AI tutor app last year. It helped me with basic algebra and geometry. It was quick and easy to use, and it gave me step-by-step explanations. It wasn’t perfect, but it was a helpful tool. I would recommend it to anyone struggling with math basics.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, make sure the equation is in standard form (ax² + bx + c = 0). Then use the quadratic formula: x equals negative b, plus or minus the square root of b squared minus four a c, all over two a. Plug in your values, calculate, and simplify. You’ll get two solutions.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "First, find the derivative of the function, y’. Then set y’ equal to zero and solve for x. Those x-values are the x-coordinates of the stationary points. Plug those values back into the original function to find the corresponding y-values. These are the points on the graph.",
